  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA thunderstorm cluster is expected to develop near northern IL\ntonight into Saturday morning that will eventually travel ESE\nthrough the lower Great Lakes in conjunction with a mid-level\nshortwave. 12z guidance appears to be coalescing around a late\nSaturday night into Sunday morning timeframe for this system in\nthe Upper Ohio River Valley, though variations in path ranging \nfrom southern OH/central WV to northeast OH/northwest PA remain.\n\nAny severe risks associated with damaging wind reside in an\nearlier arrival (Sat aftn/eve) that sees the MCS driving SE\nthrough the far eastern OH zones (Muskingum/Coshocton Counties)\nalong the CAPE gradient. Given where latest trends are heading,\nthis risk appears increasingly unlikely, especially with a\npotentially stable environment in place if it arrives later in\nthe night. More likely, the hazardous weather risk will be \nflash flooding as strong warm/moist advection (which is \npartially responsible for its eastward drive) pushes PWAT values\nnear or even exceeding the 99th percentile. Combined with warm-\nrain processes and strong ascent, rainfall rates of 1-2\" may be\ncommon (and potentially exceeded) that could result in flash \nflooding, especially on saturated grounds. The potential lack of\nnotable elevated buoyancy and faster storm motion (more on the \n25-35kts realm) could limit rainfall duration to just stay under\nFFG amounts. As mentioned, favored locations and timing remain\nvariable enough to preclude Flood Watch at this time, but could\nbe needed if confidence increases.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nStable air develops in the wake of the morning MCS Sunday that\ncould generally last most of the day and limit any activity in\nthe afternoon/evening to spotty, sub-severe coverage.\n\nHowever, there remains a low probability that enough clearing\noccurs in the wake for afternoon destabilization resulting in\nSBCAPE values rising to 1000-1800 J/kg in a 25-30kt sheared\nenvironment. If there is enough lift in the lingering NW flow, a\nfew instances of severe storms with damaging wind can't be ruled\nout along with non-zero flood risks where rain previously fell.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nScattered showers and low probability thunderstorms may linger\nwithin the increasingly stabilizing environment Sunday night\ninto Monday. However, high pressure building in from the west\nunder drier NW flow favors a drying trend in storm activity by\nMonday evening that lasts generally til Wednesday.\n\nThough timing can change, the next system featuring\nshower/thunderstorm chances favors the mid to latter week period\nnext week.",
